{-
   Taking a value **out of borrowed storage** and writing one back into it - `doc/spec/ownership.md`,
   and the rule `acc = acc ++ part` through a `&` parameter is.

   The storage is the caller's and this frame may write it, so the value in it may fill a position
   that consumes provided something is written back before the frame lets go of the reference. Two
   properties of the language make the window between the two unobservable, and both are properties
   rather than assumptions: an exclusive loan is exclusive while it is live, so no second reader
   exists to look into the hole, and nothing unwinds, so no teardown runs over one on the way out.

   Without it `pub fn (Append(a)) ++=(&target: a, ->amount: a)` in `lib/Core/Core.yana` had no body
   that could be written: `target` is a shared place under the old rule, and `++` consumes both of
   its operands.

   The goldens are the assertion, in two halves. The read of `target` is `read take` - the hole - and
   the write that follows it is `write.reinit` rather than `write.assign`, because an assignment to a
   place that holds nothing releases nothing. `Reject.BorrowRefill.yana` is the other half: a hole
   nothing fills, and a hole filled on one path only.
-}

fn joined(->a: String, ->b: String) -> String = a ++ b

-- The shape the operator is. One take and one refill, in one statement.
fn append(&target: String, ->amount: String):
    target = joined(target, amount)

-- Twice over, so the state after the first refill is what the second take reads.
fn twice(&target: String):
    target = joined(target, "cd")
    target = joined(target, "ef")

-- Emptied and refilled on both arms, which is what the meet at the join has to agree about.
fn conditional(&target: String, flag: Bool):
    if flag then target = joined(target, "gh") else target = joined(target, "ijk")

fn main() -> Int:
    let &acc = "ab" :: String
    append(acc, "12")
    twice(acc)
    conditional(acc, True)

    -- "ab" "12" "cd" "ef" "gh"
    return truncate(length(acc)) :: Int
